 IP vs MAC: IP address = your home address (can change). MAC address = your fingerprint (permanent, hardware-level).

6) Network Protocols:
---------------------
A protocol is a set of rules that allows devices to communicate effectively on a network, ensuring data is sent and received correctly. The primary protocol suite is TCP/IP.

Protocol                Layer                Purpose
TCP/IP                Layer 3 & 4         Core Internet Protocol Suite. IP routes packets; TCP ensures reliable, ordered delivery.
HTTP                  Layer 7             enables web browsing — transferring web page data between the browser and the server.
HTTPS                 Layer 7             Secure version of HTTP — encrypts data for privacy, used on sensitive sites.
FTP                   Layer 7             File Transfer Protocol — uploads/downloads files between computers on a network.
SMTP                  Layer 7             Simple Mail Transfer Protocol — sends and routes emails over the internet.

7) The OSI Model (7 Layers):
----------------------------
The OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) model defines the 7 layers that network traffic moves through between two endpoints. Data travels down the layers on the sender's side and up the layers on the receiver's side.
7 - Application: User-facing layer — where applications send/receive data. Protocols: HTTP, FTP, SMTP, DNS.
    FTP data & commands
6 - Presentation: Formats, encodes, and optionally encrypts data so both sides can understand it.
    Data encoding/Encryption
5 - Session: Manages and maintains the connection/session between sender and receiver.
    Session Management
4 - Transport: Breaks data into segments, ensures reliable delivery, handles sequencing. Protocols: TCP, UDP. Adds source/destination ports (e.g. FTP: port 21/20).
    TCP Header — ports, sequencing
3 - Network: Routes packets across networks using IP addresses. Protocol: IP. Like a GPS for your data.
    IP Header - source & destination IP
2 - Data Link: Transfers data between adjacent devices on the same network using MAC addresses. Adds frame headers and error-checking trailers.
    Frame — MAC addresses + error check
1 - Physical: Converts data into physical signals — electrical pulses (Ethernet cable) or radio waves (Wi-Fi) — for transmission over physical media.
    Electrical / Wireless signal

How data flows: Sender builds the packet from Layer 7 → Layer 1 (encapsulation). Receiver decodes from Layer 1 → Layer 7 (de-encapsulation).

8) Packets & Frames:
---------------------
Packet — Layer 3: Unit of data at the Network Layer. Contains a header with source/destination IP addresses and the actual data payload. Used when discussing IP routing.
Frame — Layer 2: Unit of data at the Data Link Layer. Contains source/destination MAC addresses and error-checking data. Encapsulates the packet for local network transmission.

All data sent over a network is divided into packets/frames. Multiple packets are transmitted and the receiving computer reassembles them in the correct order to reconstruct the original message.

9) Basic Network Devices:
-------------------------
=> Router - Directs traffic between networks and connects them to the internet. Operates at Layer 3.
=> Switch - Connects multiple devices within the same local network, managing data flow. Operates at Layer 2.
=> Modem - Connects your local network to the internet through your ISP (Internet Service Provider).

10) DNS (Domain Name System):
------------------------------
The "Phonebook" of the Internet
DNS translates human-readable domain names (like google.com) into IP addresses (like 142.250.70.78) that computers understand. Without DNS, you would need to memorize IP addresses to visit any website.

11) Basic Network Security:
----------------------------
=> Firewall - The "security guard" of your network — monitors and filters incoming/outgoing traffic, blocking unauthorized access.
=> Encryption - Scrambles data so only those with the correct key can read it. Essential for securing data in transit.
=> WPA2/WPA3 - encryption standards that protect Wi-Fi networks, ensuring only authorized users can connect.


